  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1130 PM MDT Thu Aug 6 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nAn area of high pressure will reside in the Great Basin for at\nleast another week. This will result in very warm afternoon\ntemperatures, low humidity, and little to no precipitation over\nSouthwest or North Central MT into early next week. By later next\nweek the trend is for afternoon temperatures to start to cool a\nbit, even going a bit below normal by late next week.\nAdditionally, precipitation will start to be possible from north\nto south starting late Monday. However, with the airmass very dry,\nprecipitation amounts will be very light, with any storms \ngenerally producing less than a tenth of an inch of rainfall.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nAny upper level disturbance that moves across the CWA over the\nnext week or so is expected to be a very weak disturbance. Thus\nthe chances for any precipitation are mostly less than 10 percent\nacross the CWA for the next week. A few areas have a 40 percent\nchance for a thunderstorm by late next week, but any storms that \ndo develop are going to be mostly on the dry side, producing very \nminimal precipitation.\n\nA few locations could approach record high temperatures over \nSouthwest MT today/Saturday. Brusda",
